---
/**
 * The Washington statutory framework for rental housing, rendered on every location page.
 *
 * Same reasoning as ScopeLimits: this is a citation, not prose. It was pasted into seven
 * city bodies, where it read as templated and gave the duplicate-sentence scanner a fair
 * complaint. Rewording a statute city by city would make it less accurate, not less
 * repetitive, so it lives here once and each body carries only what is genuinely local
 * about its own jurisdiction.
 */
---
<section class="legal-basis">
  <h2 style="margin-top:0;">The statutory framework, statewide</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>RCW 59.18.060(4)</dt>
    <dd>
      Requires a landlord to provide a reasonable pest control programme at the initiation of
      a tenancy and, other than in a single-family residence, to control infestation during
      the tenancy except where the tenant caused it. Two carve-outs, one sentence.
    </dd>
    <dt>RCW 59.18.130</dt>
    <dd>Tenant duties, and the route to cost liability where the tenant is the cause.</dd>
    <dt>RCW 59.18.150</dt>
    <dd>
      Entry notice. At least two days written notice to enter and inspect, one day to exhibit
      the unit, with entry permitted without consent in an emergency or on abandonment.
    </dd>
  </dl>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0;">
    Municipal rules stack on top unevenly, and most of Washington adds nothing.
    <a href="/compliance/washington-landlord-tenant-bed-bugs/">Who is responsible →</a>
    <a href="/compliance/entry-notice-for-unit-inspections/">Entry notice in detail →</a>
  </p>
</section>
